\section{Four-fermion operators and contact interactions}\label{contact}

The SM parity-violating (chiral) gauge symmetries and spontaneous/explicit breaking of these symmetries for the $W$ and $Z$ gauge boson 
masses and the hierarchy pattern of fermion masses have been at the 
centre of conceptual elaboration for possible scenarios beyond. The No-Go theorem \cite{Nielsen:1981xu, 1981PhLB..105..219N} shows that as a renormalizable quantum field theory at infrared (IR) low energies, the SM {\it bilinear} fermion Lagrangian at ultraviolet (UV) cutoff $\Lambda_{\rm cut}$ suffers theoretical inconsistencies with SM chiral gauge symmetries and fermion spectra at low energies. 
It implies that the BSM Lagrangian at $\Lambda_{\rm cut}$ should have {\it quadrilinear} operators \cite{Xue:2016dpl, Xue2017}
\begin{eqnarray}
\sum_{f=1,2,3}G_{\rm cut}\, \Big[\bar\psi^{f}_{_L}\psi^{f}_{_R}\bar\psi^{f}_{_R} \psi^{f}_{_L}\Big]_{q=0,-1,2/3,-1/3},\quad G_{\rm cut}\propto \Lambda^{-2}_{\rm cut},
\label{q1}
\end{eqnarray}
which is SM gauge symmetric four-fermion interactions of left- and right-handed fermions ($\psi^f_L,\psi^f_R$) in the SM charge sector ``$q$'' and flavour family ``$f$''. They are four-fermion operators of Nambu-Jona-Lasinio (NJL) or Einstein-Cartan type. Their origins at the scale $\Lambda_{\rm cut}$ are due to unknown grand unified interaction or quantum gravity dynamics.  

The strong four-fermion coupling $g_{\rm cut }\propto G_{\rm cut}\Lambda^2_{\rm cut}$ dynamics leads to composite bosons $\Pi^{f}\sim (\bar\psi^{f}_{_R} \psi^{f}_{_L})$ and fermions $F^{f}_R\sim \psi^{f}_{_R} (\bar\psi^{f}_{_R} \psi^{f}_{_L})\sim \psi^{f}_{_R}\Pi^{f}$ \cite{Xue1996, Xue2000, Xue2000a}. They 
are represented by the
mass (pole) and form factor (residue) of propagators, behaving as elementary particles after form-factor renormalisation. These composite particles carry SM quantum numbers and couple to massless SM gauge bosons. Their massive spectra preserve SM gauge symmetries, and constituents can 
be leptons, quarks or leptoquarks. There exists a UV fixed point $g^*_{\rm cut}$ and scaling domain, which realises an SM gauge symmetric, renormalizable {\it bilinear and dimensional-4} Lagrangian of composite bosons and fermions at the composite scale $\Lambda\ll \Lambda_{\rm cut}$ \cite{Xue2014, Xue2017}. 
As the energy scale decreases below $\Lambda$, the effective theory undergoes \cite{Xue2023} a phase transition of composite particles decay into constituents and spontaneous SM symmetries breaking via the top-quark channel $(\bar t_Lt_R\bar t_R t_L)$, realising the $\bar tt$-composite Higgs boson  
at the electroweak scale $v\approx 246$ GeV \cite{Bardeen1990}. The scale $\Lambda\approx 5.1$ TeV is estimated by using the top quark and Higgs boson masses and extrapolating the renormalization-group solution to the high-energy regime \cite{Xue2013}.

In this section we describe the four-fermion operators and contact interactions that are relevant for the study of the phenomenology of the composite bosons at $pp$ or $ep$  collisions, 
including the LHC, which will be detailed in Sec.~\ref{PhenomenologyCompositeFermions}.

We consider, among four-fermion operators (\ref{q1}) in introduction, the following SM gauge-symmetric and fermion-number conserving four-fermion operators, 
\begin{eqnarray}
&&G\left[(\bar\ell^{i}_Le_{R})(\bar d^a_{R}\psi_{Lia})
+(\bar\ell^{i}_L\nu^e_{R})(\bar u^a_{R}\psi_{Lia})\right] + {\rm h.c.},
\label{bhlql}
\end{eqnarray}
and
\begin{eqnarray}
&&G\left[(\bar\ell^{i}_L\nu^e_{R})(\bar \nu^e_{R}\ell_{Li})
+(\bar\ell^{i}_Le_{R})(\bar e_{R}\ell_{Li})\right]+ {\rm h.c.}\nonumber\\
&&G\left[(\bar\ell^{i}_Lu^a_{R})(\bar u^a_{R}\ell_{Li})
+(\bar\ell^{i}_Ld^a_{R})(\bar d^a_{R}\ell_{Li})\right]+ {\rm h.c.}
\label{bhlql1}
\end{eqnarray}
and
\begin{eqnarray}
&&G\left[(\bar\psi_{Lia}\nu^e_{R})(\bar \nu^e_{R}\psi_{Lia})
+(\bar\psi_{Lia}e_{R})(\bar e_{R}\psi_{Lia})\right] + {\rm h.c.},
\label{bhlql2}\\
&&G\left[(\bar\psi^{bi}_Lu_{Rb})(\bar u^a_{R}\psi_{Lia})+(\bar\psi^{bi}_Ld_{Rb})(\bar d^a_{R}\psi_{Lia})\right] + {\rm h.c.},
\label{qbhlql}
\end{eqnarray}
being the SM doublet $\ell^i_L=(\nu^e_L,e_L)$ and singlet $e_{R}$ with an additional right-handed neutrino $\nu^e_{R}$ for leptons;
$\psi_{Lia}=(u_{La},d_{La})$ and $u^a_{R}, d^a_{R}$ for quarks, 
where the color $a,b$ and $SU_L(2)$-isospin $i$ indexes are summed over.
Eqs. (\ref{bhlql})-(\ref{qbhlql}) 
are for the first family only, as a representative of the three fermion families.
The SM left- and right-handed fermions are mass eigenstates, their masses are negligible 
in TeV-energy regime and small mixing among three families encoded 
in $G$ is also neglected \cite{xue2016_2}.

From Eqs. (\ref{bhlql}), (\ref{bhlql1}) and (\ref{bhlql2}), we give explicitly quark-lepton interactions of three types,
\begin{eqnarray}
&&G\left[(\bar e_Le_{R})(\bar d^a_{R}d_{La})
+(\bar\nu^{e}_L\nu^e_{R})(\bar u^a_{R}u_{La})+ (\bar\nu^{e}_Le_{R})(\bar d^a_{R}u_{La})\right] + {\rm h.c.},
\label{bhlqlex}\\
&&G\left[(\bar\nu^e_Lu^a_{R})(\bar u^a_{R}\nu^e_{L})+
(\bar e_Lu^a_{R})(\bar u^a_{R}e_{L})
+(\bar\nu^e_Ld^a_{R})(\bar d^a_{R}\nu^e_{L})+
(\bar e_Ld^a_{R})(\bar d^a_{R}e_{L})\right] + {\rm h.c.},\nonumber\\
&&G\left[(\bar u^a_L\nu^e_{R})(\bar \nu^e_{R}u^a_{L})
+(\bar d^a_L\nu^e_{R})(\bar \nu^e_{R}d^a_{L})+ (\bar u^a_L e_{R})(\bar e_{R}u^a_{L})
+(\bar d^a_L e_{R})(\bar e_{R}d^a_{L})\right] + {\rm h.c.},\nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
for the first generation. $\nu^e,e,u,d \rightarrow \nu^\mu,\mu,c,s$ for the second generation, and $\nu^e,e,u,d \rightarrow \nu^\tau,\tau,t,b$ for the third generation.  


