\section{Phenomenology of the composite bosons in $pp$ and $ep$ collisions}\label{PhenomenologyCompositeFermions}

In this section we study the phenomenology of the composite fermions in $pp$ collisions. We first outline its production and decay mode and then calculate its cross section and decay width. This study leads us to the discussion on the search for $\Pi$ that will be discussed in the next section.


\subsection{Production and decay of composite bosons $\Pi^{0,\pm}$}

As already specified in Sec.~\ref{contact}, 
the composite bosons $\Pi$ can be 
\begin{eqnarray}
&&\Pi^+ ~(\bar du,\bar cs, \bar bt),\nonumber\\
&&\Pi^- ~(\bar ud,\bar sc, \bar tb),\nonumber\\
&&\Pi^0_u ~(\bar uu,\bar ss, \bar tt),\nonumber\\ 
&&\Pi^0_d ~(\bar dd,\bar cc, \bar bb),
\label{comSpecB}
\end{eqnarray}
for the charge sector $Q=+,-,0$ 
respectively, and the corresponding composite bosons 
of the higher generation of families in the SM.

The kinematics of the processes is derived in the center of mass frame of $pp$ collisions and virtual processes 
of $\Pi$ are not considered. If the energy $\sqrt{s}$ in the parton centre of mass frame is larger than composite boson masses, the following resonant process can occur:
\begin{eqnarray}
pp &\rightarrow&  ~\Pi
\label{ppfFB}
\end{eqnarray}
where the composite bosons $\Pi$ (\ref{comSpecB}) formed by Standard Model fermions 
of the higher generation of families. The kinematics of final states is simple in the centre of mass frame of $pp$ collisions.

If we neglect the quark-family mixing, the previous process can manifest at parton level as:
\begin{eqnarray}
u~\bar{d} &\rightarrow& \Pi^+,\label{udB}\\
d~\bar{u} &\rightarrow& \Pi^-,\label{udbarB}\\
u~\bar{u} &\rightarrow&  \Pi^0_u \label{uuB},\\
d~\bar{d} &\rightarrow& \Pi^0_d.\label{ddB}


\end{eqnarray}

The composite boson $\Pi$ can decay through the two different channels: two SM quarks $q q^\prime$, via the contact 
interactions in Eqs.~(\ref{ciqlB}-\ref{iqlBu}), two SM gauge bosons $\tilde G\tilde G'$ 
via the contact interactions in Eqs.~(\ref{cpg}-\ref{cpgg}). 
Then the composite fermion $\Pi$ decays as:
\begin{eqnarray}
\Pi~&\rightarrow& ~ qq^\prime ,\label{FqqB} \\
\Pi~ &\rightarrow& \tilde G\tilde G'.\label{FPB} 
\end{eqnarray}
The full decay chain is:
\begin{eqnarray}
pp &\rightarrow& \Pi  \rightarrow ~ qq^\prime ,\\
pp &\rightarrow& \Pi  \rightarrow ~ \tilde G\tilde G'.
\end{eqnarray}

It appears clear, considering all the possible flavours of $\Pi$ (\ref{comSpecB}), that a large range 
of final states is possible. 
The cross section of the process $pp \rightarrow \Pi $, the decay branching ratios of $\Pi$, and the final states and their topologies are discussed below. 

\begin{figure}[t]   
\begin{center}
\includegraphics[scale=1]{sigma_g0-5.pdf}
\caption{(Color Online) On the left panel, we show the production cross section of 
$pp\rightarrow \Pi$ as a function of $m_{_\Pi}$ for the case $g^2_{_{\rm Y}}=\left(\frac{F_\pi}{\Lambda}\right)=0.5$ and at a centre of mass energy  $\sqrt{s}=13$ TeV. The solid red line represents the results of an analytical and numerical calculation based on Eqs.~(\ref{csE0Pi}) and (\ref{csFb}) and the filled circles (black) represent the  results from our implementation of the model in CalcHEP. We find good agreement. 
On the right panel, we plot the decay width of composite boson $	\Pi$ as a function of its mass $m_{_\Pi}$ for the case $g^2_{_{\rm Y}}=\left(\frac{F_\pi}{\Lambda}\right)=0.5$. Again, we observe a good agreement between the expectation from a CalcHEP simulation and the analytical result based on Eq.~(\ref{Gamma_pqq}). 
\red{here 1. we need a plot for the decay rate; 2. we need to think the value $g^2_{_{\rm Y}}=\left(\frac{F_\pi}{\Lambda}\right)$, in connection with the previous fermion paper.}

}
\label{fig:crossSectionPi}
\end{center}

\end{figure}  

\subsection{Production and decay of quark-lepton composite bosons $\Pi^{5/3,-1/3,2/3}_a$}

As already specified in Sec.~\ref{contact}, 
the quark-lepton composite bosons $\Pi$ can be 
\begin{eqnarray}
&&\Pi^{5/3}_a ~(\bar eu,\bar \mu c, \bar\tau \replaced[id=SH]{t}{b}),\nonumber\\
&&\Pi^{-1/3}_a ~(\bar \nu^e d,\bar \nu^\mu s, \bar \nu^\mu \replaced[id=SH]{b}{t}),\nonumber\\
&&\Pi^{2/3}_{u_a} ~(\bar \nu^e u,\bar \nu^\mu c, \bar \nu^\tau \replaced[id=SH]{t}{b}),\nonumber\\ 
&&\Pi^{2/3}_{d_a} ~(\bar ed,\bar \mu s, \bar \tau b),
\label{comSpecBql}
\end{eqnarray}
for the charge sector $Q=+5/3,-1/3,2/3$ 
respectively, and the corresponding composite bosons 
of the higher generation of families in the SM.

The kinematics of the processes is derived in the center of mass frame of $pp$ collisions and virtual processes 
of $\Pi$ are not considered. If the energy $\sqrt{s}$ in the parton centre of mass frame is larger than composite boson masses, the following resonant process can occur:
\begin{eqnarray}
p~e &\rightarrow&  ~\Pi_a
\label{ppfFBql}
\end{eqnarray}
where the composite bosons $\Pi_a$ (\ref{comSpecB}) formed by Standard Model fermions 
of the higher generation of families. The kinematics of final states is simple in the centre of mass frame of $ep$ collisions.

If we neglect the quark-family mixing, the previous process can manifest at parton level as:
\begin{eqnarray}
u~\bar e &\rightarrow& \Pi^{5/3}_a,\label{udBql}\\
d~\bar\nu^e &\rightarrow& \Pi^{-1/3}_a,\label{udbarBql}\\
u~\bar\nu^e &\rightarrow&  \Pi^{2/3}_a, \label{uuBql}\\
d~\bar{e} &\rightarrow& \Pi^{2/3}_a.\label{ddBql}


\end{eqnarray}
We find that the quark-lepton composite bosons $\Pi^{5/3}_a$ and $\Pi^{2/3}_a$ can be produced by $ep$ collisions.

The composite boson $\Pi_a$ can decay through the different channels: 
SM quark $q$ and lepton $\ell$, via the contact 
interactions in Eqs.~(\ref{ciqlBql}-\ref{iqlBuql}). 
Then the composite fermion $\Pi$ decays as:
\begin{eqnarray}
\Pi_a~&\rightarrow& ~ \ell ~q ,\label{FqqBql} 
\end{eqnarray}
the branching ratio of this decay is the approximately same as 
