We turn to the pair production of composite particles (fermion and boson) by SM gauge bosons, in particularly by gluons. The composite particles are made of SM quarks and leptons. Therefore, they have SM quantum numbers to couple to SM gauge bosons. These couplings in principle are new parameters of the theory. However, If SM gauge bosons, here we particularly mean gluons,  have energies comparable to composite scale, namely the composite particle masses $M_{F,\Pi}\sim {\mathcal O}(TeV)$, then gluons interact with the quark constitute inside the composite particle. In this case, the strength of gluon coupling to composite particle can be approximately represented by QCD strong coupling $g_s=\sqrt{\alpha_s}$. This is the case for the pair productions of composite particle $\Pi, F$ and anti composite particle $\Pi^\dagger, F^\dagger$ by gluon and gluon collisions,
\begin{eqnarray}
g+g~&\rightarrow& ~ \Pi + \Pi^\dagger ~~{\rm or}~~F + F^\dagger,\label{FqqBql} 
\end{eqnarray}
The reason is that in center of mass frame, gluon and gluon collision energy must be larger than the energy threshold 
$2 M_{F,\Pi}$ to produce two composte particles flying apart, back-to-back. The composite particle and anti composite particle have the same mass. Here, we may consider the lepton-quark composite states, since their decay modes (topologies) are distinct from the SM QCD background.  But the discussions are the same for the pair productions of other composite particles in general.

This situation is very much like the Breit-Wheeler process of electron-positron pair production by light-light collision $\gamma+\gamma \rightarrow e^++e^-$. Thus, learning from the QED calculation as shown two tree-level Feynman diagrams in Fig.~\ref{ggpair}, we can write the cross-section as 
\begin{eqnarray}
\sigma_{gg\rightarrow FF^\dagger ~{\rm or}~ \Pi\Pi^\dagger}~&\approx& ~ \frac{\pi}{2}\left(\frac{\alpha_s}{M_{F,\Pi}}\right)^2(1-\hat\beta^2)
\Big[2\hat\beta^2(\hat\beta^2-2)+(3-\hat\beta^4)\ln\Big(\frac{1+\hat\beta^2}{1-\hat\beta^2}\Big)\Big],\quad \hat\beta^2=\frac{|{\bf p}|}{{\mathcal E}_{gg}}\label{ggpaircross} 
\end{eqnarray}
where ${\mathcal E}_{gg}$ is the two gluons colision energy in CM. Here we use approximation $\sigma_{gg\rightarrow FF^\dagger ~{\rm or}~ \Pi\Pi^\dagger}~\approx ...$, because we approximate the coupling between gluon and composite particle to be the QCD coupling $g_s$. For the energy ${\mathcal E}_{gg}\gg M_{F,\Pi}$, the pair-production cross-section yields
\begin{eqnarray}
\sigma_{gg\rightarrow FF^\dagger ~{\rm or}~ \Pi\Pi^\dagger}~&\approx& ~ \pi \left(\frac{\alpha_s}{M_{F,\Pi}}\right)^2\left(\frac{M_{F,\Pi}}{{\mathcal E}_{gg}}\right)^2.
\label{ggpaircrossa} 
\end{eqnarray}
Only the composite particle masses are involved, no other new parameters like $g_{_Y}$. This process should have the biggest cross section (absorption of gluons), if the gluons collision energy is over the energy threshold $M_{F,\Pi}$ of pair production of composite particles. The decay modes of back-to-back composite particles follow discussions before.  

