\subsection{Cross sections, decay widths and branching ratios}\label{xsecB}

\subsubsection{Cross sections}

The partonic cross section of  $qq^\prime\rightarrow \Pi$ is calculated by standard methods via the contact interaction in Eqs.~(\ref{ciqlB}-\ref{iqlBu}), \red{check again this equation}
\begin{eqnarray}
\hat\sigma_{_\Pi}(\hat s,m_{_\Pi})= \frac{\pi}{12}\left(\frac{F_\Pi}{\Lambda} \right)^4\delta(\hat{s}-m_{_\Pi}^2), 
\label{csE0Pi}
\end{eqnarray}  
where $\sqrt{\hat{s}}$ stands for the parton center-mass-energy of $pp$ collisions in LHC experiments. 
These are the same for all channels:
Composite bosons $\Pi^{0,\pm}$ (\ref{nB0}-\ref{uB0})
are produced in $pp$ collisions, then decay to two jets, 
\begin{eqnarray}
pp(u\bar d) &\rightarrow& \Pi^+\rightarrow {\rm two~jets}~(u \bar d)\label{pud}\\
pp(d\bar u) &\rightarrow& \Pi^-\rightarrow {\rm two~jets}~(d \bar u)\label{pdu}\\
pp (u\bar u)&\rightarrow& \Pi^0_u\rightarrow {\rm two~jets}~(u \bar u\label{puu})\\
pp (d\bar d)&\rightarrow& \Pi^0_d\rightarrow {\rm two~jets}~(d \bar d)\label{pdd}
\end{eqnarray}
as illustrated by Feynman diagram Figure \ref{compositeBv}. 
The most favorable channels in $pp$ collisions should be the productions of charged composite bosons 
$\Pi^\pm$ (\ref{nB0}). The final states are two back-to-back two standard jets (dijet). 

The cross section (\ref{csE0Pi}) should be also the same for all channels: quark-lepton
composite bosons $\Pi_a^{5/3,-1/3,2/3}$ (\ref{nB5/3}-\ref{dB2/3})
are produced in $ep$ collisions, then decay to a lepton and a jet, 
\begin{eqnarray}
ep(u\bar e) &\rightarrow& \Pi_a^{5/3}\rightarrow {\rm lepton + jet}~(u \bar e)\label{pudql}\\
ep(d\bar \nu) &\rightarrow& \Pi_a^{-1/3}\rightarrow {\rm lepton + jet}~(d \bar\nu)\label{pduql}\\
ep (u\bar \nu)&\rightarrow& \Pi_{u_a}^{2/3}\rightarrow {\rm lepton + jet}~(u \bar\nu)\label{puuql}\\
ep (d\bar e)&\rightarrow& \Pi_{d_a}^{2/3}\rightarrow  {\rm lepton + jet}~(d \bar e)\label{pddql}
\end{eqnarray}
as illustrated by Feynman diagram Figure \ref{compositeBv}. 
\red{new figure}
The most favorable channels in $ep$ collisions should be the productions of charged composite bosons 
$\Pi_a^{5/3}$ (\ref{pudql}) and $\Pi_{d_a}^{2/3}$ (\ref{pddql}). The final states are back-to-back a lepton and a standard jet. 

We consider the production cross sections for the composite fermions $F$ in $pp$ collisions expected at the LHC collider according to Feynman's parton model. 
The QCD factorization theorem allows to obtain any hadronic cross section (e.g. in $pp$ collisions) in terms of a convolution of the hard partonic cross sections $\hat\sigma$,

evaluated at the parton centre of mass energy $\sqrt{\hat s}=\sqrt{\tau s}$, with the universal parton distribution functions $f_a(x,{\hat{\cal Q}})$ which depend on the parton longitudinal momentum fractions $x$, and on the  factorization scale $\hat{\cal Q}$: \red{check again this equation}
\begin{eqnarray}
\sigma_{_\Pi}=

\sum_{ij}\!\!\intop^1_{\frac{m_{_\Pi}^{2}}{s}}\!\!d\tau \!\!\intop_\tau^1 \!\!\frac{dx}{x} f_i(x,\hat {\cal Q}^2)f_j(\frac{\tau}{x},\hat {\cal Q}^2)\hat\sigma(\tau s,m_{_\Pi})\, .
\label{csFb}
\end{eqnarray}
The factorization and renormalization scale ${\cal Q}$ is generally fixed at the value of the mass 
that is being produced.  
The parametrization of the parton distribution function is NNPDF3.0~\cite{Ball:2014uwa} and the 
factorization scale has been chosen as $\hat{\cal Q}=m_{_\Pi}$. 
The results for the four composite bosons at LHC are shown in figure (\ref{fig:crossSectionPi}), 
where we used $g_{_{\rm Y}}=\left(\frac{F_\Pi}{\Lambda}\right)^2=0.5$. 



The right panel of Fig.~\ref{fig:crossSectionPi} shows the agreement between analytical calculations based on Eqs.~(\ref{csE0Pi}) and (\ref{csFb}) 
for the case of the composite fermion $F$, and the results of simulations with CalcHEP where the model with four-fermion interactions has been implemented. 
We remark the quite good agreement that validates our model implementation in CalcHEP.


